## Kenai River

#### Beluga Observations: Visual vs. eDNA Metabarcoding vs. qPCR

eDNA and visual observations of Cook Inlet Beluga whales were largely concordant within Kenai River (Figure 6). These results demonstrate that both  metabarcoding and qPCR eDNA approaches can provide valuable data on the occurrence of belugas in the absence of visual surveys which is particularly useful when visual observations are difficult to obtain (i.e. Winter). Here routine and consistent visual surveys were not conducted in Winter and thus robust occurrence data is missing from this time series. However, eDNA data had frequent detections of beluga in the Kenai River during the Winter, strongly suggesting that beluga utilize these habitats seasonally even when the River is completely frozen over.  

