Coho and Sockeye are known prey items for Cook Inlet Belugas  [@quakenbush_diet_2015]. Traditional ecological knowledge from informal interviews with local fisherman additionally supported that the timing of the Coho run coincided with the timing of Belugas  arriving in Twentymile River both in 2021 and previous years for decades.  Thus our findings of beluga occurrence being strongly correlated with Coho run timing aligns well with previous research [@mcguire_distribution_2020; @nelson_fifty_2018; @castellote_seasonal_2020]. We note that multiple lines of evidence suggest Coho DNA was far more abundant across all time periods in Twentymile River as demonstrated by quantitative metabarcoding, bias corrected metabarcoding, qPCR, and eDNA index scores. Together, these results suggest that Cook Inlet Beluga utilization of Twentymile River in Fall is associated with the Coho run. Most importantly, these results demonstrate that weekly eDNA sampling efforts can provide sufficient resolution to capture changes in anadromous fish abundances and allow for co-occurrence analyses to inform habitat use of Cook Inlet Beluga whales.

