We find a significant difference in eDNA signatures between temporal and spatial patterns, with date sampled explaning 61.6% variance while site samples explained only 4% variance. However, sampling data failed the homogeneity of dispersions test, indicating that the variance in community composition is signficantly different across the year. August, June, and July months had far lower diveristy and a greater proportion of total reads going to a smaller number of species, particularly Sockeye salmon which has a very large annual anadromous migration during this window. We did not find a signficant difference between samples collected at the surface and at depth. Interactive effects of sampling date with both site and depth were signficant and explained 1% or less of the total variance.

These results found temporal differences across the year are greater than site differences in tidally influenced systems with a high occurrence of anadromous fishes.

