# Conclusions
Together, these results provide important baseline information needed to inform effective eDNA sampling efforts needed to resolve Cook Inlet Beluga habitat use. Specifically, these findings suggest that weekly sampling efforts at a single site along multiple important rivers would provide sufficient resolution to identify spatial and temporal river foraging habitat utilization of Cook Inlet Beluga whales. These results demonstrate that well calibrated and designed eDNA approaches are an effective tool for capturing changes of Beluga prey assemblages across both space and time. Here we recommend future efforts employ

1. a multiplexed qPCR assay that targets Beluga, King, Coho, Sockeye, Pink, Chum, and Eulachon

2. MiFish 12S metabarcoding as employed here

3. more robust mock communities targeting the 25 fish species observed in Cook Inlet

4. larger pore size sampling (5 µm) to ease sample filtration in high sediment load environments. 

Ultimately, our results demonstrate that targeted deployments of eDNA across Cook Inlet habits can reveal critical ecological insights into fish ecology and endangered Cook Inlet Beluga habitat use.

